    { id: 'ant_grasshopper', title: 'The Ant and the Grasshopper', author: "Aesop's Fables (~600 BC)", cover: '🐜', pages: [
      "All summer long the Ant worked hard, gathering grains of wheat and seeds to store in his nest for the winter. Up and down the field he went, back and forth, never stopping.",
      "The Grasshopper sat in the sun and played his fiddle and sang. Why do you work so hard, friend Ant? he laughed. Come and dance with me!",
      "I am preparing for winter, said the Ant. You should do the same. But the Grasshopper only laughed, and went on singing and dancing in the warm sun.",
      "Summer ended. The leaves fell. Cold winds began to blow. The Grasshopper had nothing to eat. He was hungry and shivering.",
      "He came to the Ant's nest and begged for food. The Ant looked at him sternly. You sang all summer, the Ant said. Now you must dance all winter.",
      "The moral: there is a time for work and a time for play — prepare today for what you will need tomorrow."
    ]},
    { id: 'town_country_mouse', title: 'The Town Mouse and the Country Mouse', author: "Aesop's Fables (~600 BC)", cover: '🐭', pages: [
      "A Town Mouse went to visit his cousin, the Country Mouse. The Country Mouse welcomed him warmly and shared his simple meal — barley, roots, and acorns.",
      "The Town Mouse turned up his nose. Is THIS what you eat? In the town we have cheese and cake and honey every day! Come back with me and see for yourself.",
      "The Country Mouse agreed. They travelled to the great house in town, and the Town Mouse led him into a fine dining room. Half-eaten cakes, jellies, and meats were laid on the table.",
      "The Country Mouse was amazed. He had never seen such a feast. He began to nibble eagerly.",
      "Suddenly the door burst open. In rushed a great cat with sharp claws. The two mice fled to a tiny hole in the wall, hearts pounding.",
      "When the cat had gone, the Country Mouse picked up his little bundle. Goodbye, cousin. You may have your cakes and your danger. I would rather eat barley in peace.",
      "The moral: a simple meal in peace is better than a feast in fear."
    ]},
    { id: 'princess_pea', title: 'The Princess and the Pea', author: 'Hans Christian Andersen (1835)', cover: '👑', pages: [
      "Once upon a time there was a Prince who wanted to marry a real princess — but she had to be a real one. He travelled the whole world looking, but somehow each one had a flaw. He returned home very sad.",
      "One stormy night, there came a knocking at the city gate. The old King himself opened it. Outside stood a girl, soaked through with rain. Her hair clung to her face and her clothes dripped, but she said she was a real princess.",
      "Well, we shall see about that, said the old Queen. She said nothing else, but went to the bedroom, took all the bedclothes off, and placed a single pea on the bottom of the bed.",
      "Then she piled twenty mattresses on top of the pea, and twenty eider-down beds on top of the mattresses. There the princess was to sleep that night.",
      "In the morning, the King and Queen asked how she had slept. Oh, terribly! said the princess. Hardly a wink! There was something so hard in the bed that I am black and blue all over.",
      "Then they knew she was a real princess, because no one but a real princess could feel a pea through twenty mattresses and twenty eider-downs. The Prince took her for his wife, for now he knew he had a real princess. The pea was put in the museum, where it can still be seen if no one has taken it. The end."
    ]},
    { id: 'emperors_clothes', title: "The Emperor's New Clothes", author: 'Hans Christian Andersen (1837)', cover: '👔', pages: [
      "Once there lived an Emperor who loved fine clothes more than anything else. He spent all his money on them. He had a different outfit for every hour of the day.",
      "One day two swindlers came to the city. They said they could weave the most magnificent cloth ever seen — a cloth so fine that anyone who was stupid or unfit for his job would not be able to see it at all.",
      "The Emperor paid them a fortune. The swindlers set up looms and pretended to weave, but their looms were empty.",
      "The Emperor sent his oldest minister to check on the work. The poor minister stared at the empty looms and saw nothing. But he dared not say so, for fear of being thought a fool. Beautiful! he cried. Such patterns! Such colors!",
      "Soon the whole court came to admire the invisible cloth. None of them could see anything, but none of them dared say so.",
      "At last the swindlers pretended to dress the Emperor in the new clothes. The Emperor saw nothing in the mirror but his own bare skin, but he too was afraid to admit it. So he walked out into the city for a grand procession.",
      "All the people cheered the wonderful new clothes that they could not see — until a small child cried out, But he hasn't got anything on!",
      "The whisper spread through the crowd. The Emperor knew the child was right, but he held his head high and walked on, while his servants carried the train of robes that did not exist. The moral: sometimes only a child has the courage to speak the truth."
    ]},
    { id: 'cinderella', title: 'Cinderella', author: 'Charles Perrault (1697)', cover: '👠', pages: [
      "Once upon a time there was a gentleman who married for his second wife the proudest and haughtiest woman ever known. She had two daughters who were just like her. The gentleman had a young daughter of his own who was as kind and gentle as her stepmother and stepsisters were cruel.",
      "The stepmother made the kind daughter do all the hardest work in the house. When her chores were done, she would sit by the chimney corner among the cinders, which is why everyone called her Cinderella.",
      "It happened that the King's son was to give a great ball, and invited all the fine ladies in the kingdom. The two stepsisters were invited. They tried on their finest dresses and made Cinderella curl their hair. Cinderella did everything they asked, but she longed to go to the ball too.",
      "When her family had gone, Cinderella sat by the fire and wept. Suddenly her Fairy Godmother appeared. Why are you crying, child? I — I wish I could go to the ball, said Cinderella.",
      "The Fairy Godmother turned a pumpkin into a fine coach, six mice into white horses, a rat into a coachman, and Cinderella's rags into a gown of silver and gold, with a pair of glass slippers. But you must be home by midnight, said the Fairy. At twelve o'clock everything will return to what it was.",
      "At the ball, the Prince thought she was the most beautiful lady he had ever seen. They danced together all evening. Cinderella forgot the time — and then she heard the clock begin to strike twelve! She fled down the palace steps, losing one glass slipper on the way.",
      "The Prince picked up the tiny glass slipper. He sent his messengers all through the kingdom: every maiden must try it on, and whoever it fits will be his bride. The slipper was too large for the stepsisters' feet. Then Cinderella stepped forward. Let me try, she said softly. It slid on perfectly.",
      "The Prince knew her at once. He took her to the palace and married her, and Cinderella, who was as good as she was beautiful, forgave her stepsisters and brought them to court. They all lived happily ever after. The end."
    ]},
    { id:'gift_magi', title:'The Gift of the Magi', author:"O. Henry (1905)", cover:'🎁', pages:[
      "One dollar and eighty-seven cents. That was all. And sixty cents of it was in pennies — pennies saved one and two at a time by bulldozing the grocer and the butcher. Three times Della counted it. One dollar and eighty-seven cents. And the next day would be Christmas. There was clearly nothing left to do but flop down on the shabby little couch and howl.",
      "Della and Jim Young were a young couple living in a furnished flat at eight dollars a week. Jim's salary had shrunk from thirty dollars a week to twenty, and yet they tried to make a happy home together. Tomorrow was Christmas, and she had only $1.87 with which to buy Jim a present.",
      "Now, there were two possessions of the Young family in which they both took mighty pride. One was Jim's gold watch that had been his father's and his grandfather's. The other was Della's long, beautiful hair, which fell about her in rippling, shining brown cascades almost to her knees.",
      "Suddenly Della whirled from the window. Her eyes were shining brilliantly, but her face had lost its color within twenty seconds. She let down her hair, looked at herself in the mirror, then put on her old brown jacket and old brown hat, and went out to a sign that read: 'Mme. Sofronie. Hair Goods of All Kinds.'",
      "'Will you buy my hair?' asked Della. 'I buy hair,' said Madame. 'Take your hat off and let's have a sight at the looks of it.' Down rippled the brown cascade. 'Twenty dollars,' said Madame, lifting the mass with a practised hand. 'Give it to me quick,' said Della.",
      "For two hours Della searched the stores. She found it at last. It was a platinum fob chain, simple and chaste in design, properly proclaiming its value by substance alone. As soon as she saw it she knew that it must be Jim's. It was like him — quietness and value. She paid twenty-one dollars for it, and hurried home with the eighty-seven cents.",
      "When Jim came home and saw her short hair, he stopped just inside the door and stared. Della rushed to him. 'Jim, darling, don't look at me that way. I had my hair cut off and sold it because I couldn't have lived through Christmas without giving you a present. It'll grow out again — you won't mind, will you?'",
      "Jim sat down on the couch and drew her into his arms. 'Dell,' said he, 'let's put our Christmas presents away and keep 'em a while. They're too nice to use just at present.' He drew a package from his overcoat pocket and threw it on the table. 'I sold the watch to get the money to buy your combs,' he said.",
      "Della opened the package. The combs — the set of combs, side and back, that Della had worshipped for long in a Broadway window. Beautiful combs, pure tortoise shell, with jewelled rims — just the shade to wear in the beautiful vanished hair. But she only hugged them to her bosom, and at length was able to look up with dim eyes and a smile and say: 'My hair grows so fast, Jim!'",
      "The magi, as you know, were wise men — wonderfully wise men — who brought gifts to the Babe in the manger. They invented the art of giving Christmas presents. Of all who give and receive gifts, such as these two are the wisest. Everywhere they are wisest. They are the magi."
    ]},
    { id:'last_leaf', title:'The Last Leaf', author:"O. Henry (1907)", cover:'🍂', pages:[
      "In a little district west of Washington Square the streets have run crazy and broken themselves into small strips called 'places.' These 'places' make strange angles and curves. In November a cold, unseen stranger called Pneumonia stalked about the colony, touching one here and there with his icy fingers.",
      "Mr. Pneumonia struck down Johnsy, a frail little girl from California who had come to live with Sue, her painter friend, in a brick studio at the top of an old building. The doctor took Sue into the hallway and said privately, 'She has one chance in ten. And that chance is for her to want to live.'",
      "Johnsy lay in bed, scarcely moving, looking out the small window at the side of a brick house twenty feet away. An old, old ivy vine, gnarled and decayed at the roots, climbed half way up the wall. The cold autumn winds had stripped the leaves from the vine until its skeleton branches clung, almost bare, to the crumbling bricks.",
      "'Six,' Johnsy said in almost a whisper. 'They're falling faster now. Three days ago there were almost a hundred. Five.' 'Five what, dear?' said Sue. 'Leaves. On the ivy vine. When the last one falls I must go too. I've known that for three days.'",
      "Sue went downstairs to fetch Old Behrman, a painter who lived on the ground floor. Behrman was an old man past sixty who had been a failure in art. For forty years he had been about to paint a masterpiece, yet had never begun it. He earned a little by serving as a model to young artists. He drank gin to excess, and still talked of his coming masterpiece.",
      "Sue told Behrman about Johnsy and her fancy. The old man's red eyes streamed at his own helplessness. 'Vass!' he cried. 'Is dere people in de world mit der foolishness to die because leafs dey drop off from a confounded vine? Some day I vill paint a masterpiece, and ve shall all go away.' He climbed the stairs with her and stood at the window. They looked out fearfully at the few remaining leaves. Then they sat together while the cold rain fell, mingled with snow.",
      "When morning came Johnsy commanded that the shade be raised. There was one leaf left on the ivy vine. Dark green near its stem, with its serrated edges tinted yellow, it hung bravely from a branch some twenty feet above the ground. 'It is the last one,' said Johnsy. 'I thought it would surely fall during the night. I shall go today.'",
      "But the day wore away, and as twilight came they could see the lone leaf clinging to its branch against the wall. And then, with the coming of the night, the north wind raged again. When it was light enough Johnsy demanded that the shade be raised. The ivy leaf was still there.",
      "Johnsy lay for a long time looking at it. 'Sue,' she said. 'I've been a bad girl. Something has made that last leaf stay there to show me how wicked I was. It is a sin to want to die. Bring me a little broth now.' That afternoon the doctor said, 'Out of danger. You've won.'",
      "Later Sue came to the bed where Johnsy lay and put her arm around her. 'I have something to tell you, white mouse,' she said. 'Mr. Behrman died of pneumonia today in the hospital. He was ill only two days. They found him on the morning of the first day in his room helpless with pain. His shoes and clothing were wet through and icy cold. They couldn't imagine where he had been on such a dreadful night. And then they found a lantern still lighted, and a ladder dragged from its place, and some scattered brushes, and a palette with green and yellow colors mixed on it — Johnsy, dear, look out the window at the last ivy leaf. Didn't you wonder why it never fluttered when the wind blew? Ah, darling, it's Behrman's masterpiece — he painted it there the night the last leaf fell.'"
    ]},
    { id:'three_questions', title:'The Three Questions', author:"Leo Tolstoy (1885)", cover:'❓', pages:[
      "It once occurred to a certain king that if he always knew the right time to begin everything, if he knew who were the right people to listen to and whom to avoid, and above all, if he always knew what was the most important thing to do, he would never fail in anything he might undertake.",
      "He had it proclaimed throughout his kingdom that he would give a great reward to anyone who would teach him three things: what was the right time for every action, who were the most necessary people, and what was the most important thing to do at any moment.",
      "Learned men came to the king, but they all answered his questions differently. Some said the king should draw up a table of days and live strictly by it. Others said he should keep a council of wise men. Still others insisted the most important thing was war, or science, or religious worship. All the answers being different, the king agreed with none of them, and gave the reward to no one.",
      "He decided to consult a hermit, widely renowned for his wisdom. The hermit lived in a wood which he never left, and he received none but common folk. So the king put on simple clothes, and before reaching the cell dismounted from his horse and went on alone.",
      "When the king approached, the hermit was digging the ground in front of his hut. He was old and weak, and at each stroke of the spade he breathed heavily. The king said, 'I have come to ask you three questions: How can I learn to do the right thing at the right time? Who are the people I most need? And what affairs are most important?'",
      "The hermit listened, but answered nothing. He just spat on his hand and recommenced digging. Seeing he was tired, the king said, 'You are weary. Let me take the spade and work in your stead.' 'Thanks,' said the hermit, and gave the king the spade. The king dug one bed and then another. The sun began to sink.",
      "Suddenly they saw a bearded man come running out of the wood. His hands were pressed against his stomach, and blood was flowing from beneath them. When he reached the king, he fainted. The king and the hermit tended his wound, washing it and dressing it with a handkerchief and a towel. The man drank some water and revived.",
      "The king brought fresh water from a stream and they laid the wounded man on the hermit's bed. In the morning the man said to the king, 'Forgive me. You do not know me, but I know you. I swore vengeance against you because you executed my brother and seized his property. I came to kill you on your return home, but you stayed with the hermit. I lay in ambush, but your guards recognized me and wounded me. Now you have saved my life. If I live, I will serve you as your most faithful servant.'",
      "The king was glad to have made peace with an enemy so easily. He turned to the hermit. 'For the last time I pray you to answer my questions.' 'You have already been answered,' said the hermit. 'Had you not pitied my weakness yesterday and dug these beds for me, you would have been attacked on your way back. So the most important time was when you were digging the beds, the most important man was I, and the most important affair was to do me good.'",
      "'Later, when the wounded man ran to us, the most important time was when you attended to him; the most important man was he; and the most important affair was the care you bestowed on him. Remember then: there is only one time that is important — Now! It is the most important time because it is the only time when we have any power. The most necessary man is he with whom you are, for no one knows whether he will ever have dealings with anyone else. And the most important affair is to do that man good, because for that purpose alone was man sent into this life.'"
    ]}
